1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,200
If you're constantly losing focus at your desk, forgetting what you walked into a room for, or rereading the same email three times, brain fog is probably why — and it eats into how much you actually get done.

2
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,160
Brain fog isn't a diagnosis on its own.

3
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:22,080
It's a cluster of symptoms: trouble concentrating, losing your train of thought, and blanking on the right word, often all at once.

4
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,920
The most common trigger doctors point to is blood sugar swings.

5
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:35,680
Your brain runs almost entirely on glucose, so when your blood sugar drops too low between meals, it doesn't get the steady fuel it needs, and that's when the fog rolls in.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,160
Sleep debt, chronic stress, and hormonal shifts during pregnancy or menopause stack right on top of that.

7
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:47,440
Three fixes make the biggest difference: steady meals, protected sleep, and real breaks.

8
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:49,600
First, stop skipping meals.

9
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:55,680
A protein-and-fiber breakfast keeps your blood sugar level instead of spiking and crashing by mid-morning.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,960
Second, protect seven-plus hours of sleep instead of trading it for one more episode.

11
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,240
Sleep debt hits focus before it hits anything else.

12
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:08,480
And finally, build in a real screen-free break every couple of hours.

13
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,360
Even five minutes away from your screen resets your focus better than another cup of coffee does.

14
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:17,360
Brain fog isn't a personality flaw or a productivity failure.

15
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:22,160
Steady your blood sugar, protect your sleep, and take real breaks — the fog clears.
